Package sources for the host tests. tests/make_packages.py builds them with tools/nbtpkg.py, signs them with the
test key, and derives the deliberately broken packages from hello-1.2.0. Nothing here runs on a car: the programs
are shell scripts that stand in for binaries.

  runtime-2.1.0          a folder entry; hello depends on it
  runtime-3.0.0          the same, one major version on: outside the range hello asks for, so a plan that
                         upgrades to it while hello is installed must be refused
  hello-1.1.0, -1.2.0    files, a config file, a link and all four hooks; 1.2.0 adds a file
  hello-1.3.0-failing    an upgrade whose postInstall exits with 3, so it must be undone
  slow-1.0.0             a postInstall that outlives its timeout
  prefail-1.0.0          a preInstall that exits with 7
  plugin-1.0.0           an HMI plugin folder with a connector id (250) and a focus id (32760)
  patch-1.0.0, -1.1.0    replaces the stock file /opt/demo/menu.xml (the tests create it: "stock menu, firmware A"
                         or "... B" and a newline), with a config file in ${data}; patch-2.0.0 lets go of it again
  syshook-1.0.0          a file and a link on the system partition and all four hooks, each of which prints its
                         name: the tests look at the partition while a hook runs. Its postInstall exits with 3
                         if ${data}/fail exists, for an install that is undone after the hook
  rival-1.0.0            conflicts with hello below 2.0.0, and its preRemove fails
  rival2-1.0.0           wants the path rival owns
  noisy-1.0.0            a postInstall that prints 15 KiB, with characters of several bytes and one byte that is
                         not UTF-8: what job.log has to cut into pieces and repair
  longname-1.0.0         a name of 64 characters, some of them outside ASCII: the longest a list entry gets
  (no folder here)       org.nbtapp.plugin 0.9.0, 1.0.0 and 1.1.0, the iDrive plugin's own package: tests/make_packages.py
                         has tools/plugin_pkg.py make them, as it makes the one for a head unit, of the manifest
                         and UI description in plugin/ and stand-ins for the library and the two DBs
  radio-1.0.0            declares two Bluetooth serial services, both registered by its program radiod (a script
                         that registers nothing): what the agent removes when that program is not running
  radio2-1.0.0           declares the first of radio's two services for a program of its own, so the two cannot
                         both be installed
  agent-1.1.0            the agent's own package, org.nbtapp.agent: ${app}/nbtagent, ${app}/nbtctl and the
                         version's launcher, ${app}/nbtlaunch.sh. Its nbtagent answers --version only, and its
                         launcher is read by the shell and never run. The launcher's test, which has to start
                         the new version, makes its own package of the test bench's second build of the agent
  agent-1.2.0            the same one version on, with a file more; its nbtagent answers --version only
  agent-2.0.0-misnamed   its nbtagent says it is 2.0.1                    } what an install of the agent's own
  agent-2.1.0-broken     its nbtagent exits with 3                        } package refuses: these three once
  agent-2.5.0-badlauncher  its launcher is an if with no fi               } they are placed, when the program
                                                                            is asked for its version and the
                                                                            shell reads the launcher,
  agent-2.2.0-noprogram  places nbtagent with mode 0644                   } and these three by the manifest
  agent-2.3.0-outside    places a file outside ${app} and ${data}         }
  agent-2.4.0-nolauncher places no ${app}/nbtlaunch.sh                    }
